.Dd October 30, 2026
.Dt SDBX 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sdbx
.Nd OS-level sandbox for agents and tools
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm run
.Op Ar flags
.Fl \-
.Ar command
.Op Ar arg ...
.Nm
.Ar command
.Op Ar arg ...
.Nm
.Cm shell
.Op Ar flags
.Nm
.Cm check
.Op Ar flags
.Nm
.Cm policy
.Op Ar flags
.Op Fl \-format Cm yaml | json | seatbelt | bwrap
.Nm
.Cm explain
.Op Ar flags
.Ar path | host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
.Nm
.Cm learn
.Op Ar flags
.Fl \-
.Ar command
.Op Ar arg ...
.Nm
.Cm init
.Op Ar init-flags
.Nm
.Cm doctor
.Nm
.Cm presets
.Nm
.Cm version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs a command inside an operating-system sandbox.
It is meant for coding agents, package managers and build tools that
should not get full access to your home directory, secrets and network.
.Pp
The sandbox has four goals:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Integrity: writes only in the workspace and in explicitly allowed paths.
.It
Confidentiality: no SSH keys, cloud credentials, browser profiles or
secret environment variables.
.It
Network: outbound traffic only through a proxy with a domain allowlist.
.It
Persistence: no Git hooks, shell rc files, autostart entries,
programs in
.Pa ~/bin
or
.Pa ~/.local/bin ,
and no changes to the sdbx configuration.
.El
.Pp
On macOS
.Nm
uses Seatbelt
.Pq Xr sandbox-exec 1 ,
on Linux
.Xr bwrap 1
together with Landlock and seccomp.
If a rule cannot be enforced on the platform,
.Nm
does not start
.Pq fail-closed ,
unless
.Fl \-degraded
is given.
.Pp
The workspace is the Git root of the current directory, otherwise the
current directory.
When a run ends,
.Nm
terminates every process created inside the sandbox, including detached ones
.Pq Ic nohup , setsid .
.Pp
Messages printed by
.Nm
are currently in German.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm run Oo Ar flags Oc Fl \- Ar command Op Ar arg ...
Runs
.Ar command
in the sandbox.
The command's exit code is passed through
.Pq see Sx EXIT STATUS .
.It Ar command Op Ar arg ...
Short for
.Nm
.Cm run
.Fl \-
.Ar command ... ,
provided
.Ar command
is not an sdbx command and is found in
.Ev PATH .
sdbx flags cannot be used in the short form.
.It Cm shell Op Ar flags
Starts
.Ev SHELL
.Pq or Pa /bin/sh
in the sandbox.
.It Cm check Op Ar flags
Loads and validates the configuration, prints warnings and the matrix of
rules enforceable on this platform.
Starts nothing and creates nothing.
.It Cm policy Oo Ar flags Oc Op Fl \-format Ar format
Prints the resolved policy.
.Ar format
is
.Cm yaml
.Pq default ,
.Cm json ,
.Cm seatbelt
.Pq the generated Seatbelt profile
or
.Cm bwrap
.Pq the bwrap arguments .
.It Cm explain Oo Ar flags Oc Ar path | host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
Shows which rule applies to a path or network destination, and the file
and line it comes from.
.It Cm learn Oo Ar flags Oc Fl \- Ar command Op Ar arg ...
Learning mode: runs the command, collects blocked accesses and suggests
configuration entries.
Nothing is written.
.It Cm init Op Ar init-flags
Creates a commented project configuration in
.Pa ~/.config/sdbx/projects/ .
A small TUI asks for anything not given as a flag; without a terminal,
.Cm init
aborts unless
.Fl \-yes
is given.
See
.Sx INIT FLAGS .
.It Cm doctor
Checks platform prerequisites
.Pq sandbox-exec, bwrap, Landlock, user namespaces, cgroups .
.It Cm presets
Lists the built-in presets and integrations.
.It Cm version
Prints version, commit and the detected platform capabilities.
.El
.Sh FLAGS
These apply to
.Cm run ,
.Cm shell ,
.Cm check ,
.Cm policy ,
.Cm explain
and
.Cm learn .
Flags marked
.Dq repeatable
may be given more than once.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c , Fl \-config Ar file
Load
.Ar file
as the project override instead of looking one up.
If it contains
.Ic project: ,
that sets the workspace.
.It Fl \-no-global
Ignore the global configuration.
.It Fl w , Fl \-workspace Ar dir
Override the workspace.
.It Fl \-allow-write Ar path
Additional writable path (repeatable).
.It Fl \-allow-read Ar path
Additional readable path (repeatable).
.It Fl \-deny Ar path
Additionally block a path (repeatable).
.It Fl \-allow-domain Ar pattern
Allow a domain, e.g.\&
.Ql *.example.com
or
.Ql api.example.com:443
(repeatable).
.It Fl \-no-network
No network.
.It Fl \-integration Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
Configure an integration, e.g.\&
.Ql clausona=ro
or
.Ql clausona.profiles=all
(repeatable).
.It Fl \-timeout Ar duration
Time limit, e.g.\&
.Ql 30m .
The process tree then receives
.Dv SIGTERM ,
and
.Dv SIGKILL
after
.Ic limits.kill_grace .
.It Fl \-degraded
Only warn about unenforceable rules instead of aborting.
.It Fl \-log Ar file
Write an audit log.
.It Fl \-log-format Cm text | json
Audit log format.
.It Fl q , Fl \-quiet
Suppress warnings.
.It Fl v , Fl \-verbose
More verbose output.
.It Fl \-dry-run
Only print the launch description, run nothing.
.It Fl \-keep-tmp
Keep the run's private temporary directory.
.It Fl \-no-rewrite
Don't let integrations rewrite the command line.
.El
.Sh INIT FLAGS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl \-preset Ar name
Include a preset (repeatable);
.Cm none
for no presets.
.It Fl \-integration Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
Enable an integration (repeatable).
.It Fl \-agents Ar list
Without clausona: agent permissions for
.Cm claude ,
.Cm codex
or
.Cm none .
.It Fl \-allow-write Ar path , Fl \-allow-domain Ar pattern , Fl \-allow-socket Ar path , Fl \-deny Ar path
Additional entries (repeatable).
.It Fl \-network Cm proxy | none | host
Network mode.
.It Fl \-workspace Ar dir
Target workspace (default: Git root, otherwise the current directory).
.It Fl \-global
Create the global configuration
.Pa ~/.config/sdbx/config.yaml
instead of a project override.
.It Fl \-output Ar file
Different target file.
If it lies inside the workspace,
.Nm
only loads it with
.Fl \-config .
.It Fl \-yes
Accept all suggestions, ask nothing.
.It Fl \-force
Replace an existing file without asking; the old one is kept as
.Pa <file>.bak .
.It Fl \-accessible
Accessible mode with line prompts; also via
.Ev SDBX_ACCESSIBLE Ns =1 .
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration lives outside the workspace so the sandbox cannot modify it.
Sources are merged in this order; later sources win for single values,
lists are combined:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
built-in defaults,
.It
global:
.Pa ~/.config/sdbx/config.yaml ,
.It
enabled integrations,
.It
project override from
.Pa ~/.config/sdbx/projects/ ,
.It
command-line flags.
.El
.Pp
A project override is a file with any name in
.Pa projects/
containing
.Ic project: Ar workspace-path .
The file whose
.Ic project:
matches the current workspace applies; subdirectories inherit it.
Files without
.Ic project:
are skipped and can serve as shared include fragments.
If several files match, that is an error.
.Pp
In an override, relative paths in entries and
.Li ${CONFIG_DIR}
resolve against the workspace;
.Ic include:
paths are relative to the file itself.
A
.Pa sdbx.yaml
or
.Pa .sdbx.yaml
in the workspace is not loaded, only reported, and stays write-protected.
.Pp
Example
.Pa ~/.config/sdbx/projects/myapp.yaml :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
version: 1
project: ~/Code/myapp
include: [node, git-https]
filesystem:
  write: [~/.cache/my-tool]
  deny: ["**/.env"]
network:
  allow: [api.example.com, "*.cdn.example.com"]
integrations:
  clausona: ro
.Ed
.Pp
Built-in presets:
.Cm git-https ,
.Cm go ,
.Cm node ,
.Cm python ,
.Cm rust .
The full schema is in
.Pa docs/spezifikation.md ,
section 4.2 (German).
.Sh PROTECTED PATHS
The following paths stay write-protected even if a writable area covers
them or names them exactly:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
in the workspace and Git repositories:
.Pa .git/hooks ,
.Pa .git/config ,
.Pa .git/info ,
.Pa .gitmodules ,
.Pa .envrc ,
editor and agent settings
.Pq Pa .vscode , .idea , .cursor , .claude/settings*.json , .mcp.json , ... ;
.It
in the home directory: shell rc files,
.Pa ~/.gitconfig ,
.Pa ~/.ssh/config ,
autostart
.Pq systemd user units, LaunchAgents ,
.Pa ~/.config/sdbx/ ,
.Pa ~/bin ,
.Pa ~/.local/bin ,
.Pa ~/.local/share/bin ;
.It
every directory in
.Ev PATH
outside the workspace.
.El
.Pp
Protection can only be lifted with
.Ic filesystem.unprotect
(with a warning).
Exception: an exactly matching
.Ic write
entry unlocks the bin directories in the home directory, also with a warning.
Missing protected home directories below a writable area are created by
.Nm
before the start, so the sandbox cannot create and fill them itself.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Ss Outside the sandbox
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If set, the configuration lives in
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sdbx/
.Pq on macOS too ;
that directory is then protected as well.
.It Ev SHELL
Shell used by
.Cm shell .
.It Ev SDBX_ACCESSIBLE
.Ql 1
enables the accessible mode of
.Cm init .
.El
.Ss Inside the sandbox
By default only harmless variables are passed through
.Pq Ev PATH , HOME , TERM , LANG , LC_* , ... ;
variables such as
.Ev *_TOKEN ,
.Ev *_API_KEY ,
.Ev AWS_*
or
.Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK
are always removed.
Always set:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev SDBX
.Ql 1 .
A nested
.Nm
uses this to detect that it already runs inside a sandbox.
.It Ev SDBX_VERSION
Version of
.Nm .
.It Ev SDBX_WORKSPACE
Absolute path of the workspace.
.It Ev TMPDIR
Private temporary directory of this run.
.It Ev HTTP_PROXY , Ev HTTPS_PROXY , Ev ALL_PROXY , Ev NO_PROXY
(also lowercase) Proxy address
.Pq HTTP or SOCKS5
and localhost exceptions, if
.Ic network.mode
is
.Cm proxy .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/sdbx/config.yaml
Global configuration.
.It Pa ~/.config/sdbx/projects/*.yaml
Project overrides.
.It Pa /tmp/sdbx-<run-id>/
Linux: private
.Pa /tmp
of a run, deleted afterwards
.Pq unless Fl \-keep-tmp .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0\(en123
Exit code of the command, unchanged.
.It 124
Time limit reached.
.It 125
Configuration or policy error; nothing was started.
.It 126
The sandbox could not be set up
.Pq missing platform capability, unenforceable rule .
.It 127
Command not found.
.It 128+ Ns Ar n
Command terminated by signal
.Ar n .
.El
.Pp
Codes 124\(en127 can also come from the command itself.
In those cases
.Nm
always prints a line prefixed with
.Ql sdbx:
to stderr.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start an agent in the sandbox:
.Dl $ sdbx claude
.Pp
Run a build without network:
.Dl $ sdbx run --no-network -- make test
.Pp
Create and check a project configuration interactively:
.Dl $ sdbx init
.Dl $ sdbx check
.Pp
Find out why an access is blocked:
.Dl $ sdbx explain ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
.Dl $ sdbx explain registry.npmjs.org:443
.Pp
Find the permissions a command is missing:
.Dl $ sdbx learn -- npm install
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bwrap 1 ,
.Xr sandbox-exec 1 ,
.Xr seccomp 2 ,
.Xr landlock 7
.Pp
.Pa docs/spezifikation.md
in the source tree contains the full specification (German).
.Sh CAVEATS
.Bl -bullet
.It
No protection against kernel exploits or side channels.
.It
Exfiltration through explicitly allowed domains remains possible.
.It
On Linux, globs, socket masking and protected paths only apply to paths
that exist at start.
If protected paths are created inside the sandbox,
.Nm
reports this after the run.
.It
Repositories created during a run are reported;
their Git config can still be modified before the next run.
.It
In the restricted Linux mode
.Pq Ic linux.backend : Cm landlock ,
protected paths inside writable areas cannot be enforced;
this mode requires
.Fl \-degraded .
.It
The macOS keychain can only be allowed as a whole.
.El
